					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-215976#post-2488853</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-215976/of-multiverses-and-dandelion-wine#post-2488853</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Sat, 09 Apr 2016 22:37:10 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Agent MacLeod</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1922529</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <div class="collapsible-block"> <div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Collapsed&nbsp;to&nbsp;hide&nbsp;spoilers</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none"> <div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div> <div class="collapsible-block-content"> <p>I have no idea why I've never commented on this, but here's to hoping future-me doesn't read this comment and think, &quot;Oh Great Cthulhu, what the hell was wrong with me back then?&quot;</p> <p>This is absolutely one of my favorite tales on the whole site, and probably one of my favorite pieces of fiction anywhere. It's terrifying, but beautiful. It gets me every time. The idea of someone desperately fighting for life while drifting through the multiverse <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-507">SCP-507</a>-style, while also trying to find and desperately hoping for someone or something that can save them, something that can save <em>everything</em>, just&#8230; I don't know. The fact that he seems completely without hope somehow makes it that much more moving.</p> <p>Question merely for clarification: Is this about <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/mackenzie-s-proposal">Mackenzie's Proposal</a>, or is the similarity merely superficial?</p> </div> </div> </div> <p>As stated above, this is one of my favorites. I've already read it multiple times and will likely read it again, because this is the sort of thing you don't just read once.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
